# =============================================================================
# SECRETS -- the most important section in this file.
#
# A secret committed to git is compromised permanently. Deleting it in a later
# commit does NOT help: the value stays in history, and automated scanners
# harvest public pushes within seconds. Recovery means rotating the credential,
# not editing the repo.
# =============================================================================
#
# SYNTAX WARNING, learned the hard way:
# A `#` only starts a comment at the START of a line. A trailing `#` becomes
# part of the PATTERN, so `*.tfvars   # holds passwords` silently matches
# nothing and ignores nothing. No error is raised -- you discover it when a
# secret is already public. Keep every comment on its own line.
#
.env
.env.*
# ...but the TEMPLATE is committed: it documents setting NAMES, never values.
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
# Public keys are safe by definition.
!**/*.pub
secrets.yaml
credentials.json
# Terraform variable files routinely hold database passwords and API keys.
*.tfvars
!example.tfvars
!terraform.tfvars.example

# =============================================================================
# Python
# =============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
ENV/

# =============================================================================
# Testing and coverage artefacts -- generated output, never source
# =============================================================================
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.ruff_cache/

# =============================================================================
# Terraform
# =============================================================================
# Provider binaries: hundreds of MB, re-downloaded by `terraform init`.
.terraform/
# STATE FILES. Two independent reasons to never commit these:
#   1. State contains every attribute of every resource, including generated
#      passwords and connection strings, in PLAINTEXT.
#   2. State must be shared and LOCKED across a team. Git cannot lock, so two
#      concurrent applies would silently corrupt each other's view of reality.
#      Real teams use a remote backend (S3 + DynamoDB, Terraform Cloud).
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform.lock.hcl.bak
crash.log

# =============================================================================
# Local application data
# =============================================================================
*.db
*.sqlite3

# =============================================================================
# Editors and OS noise
# =============================================================================
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
